  "articleBody": "Basement door installations fail most often because of moisture issues and hasty rough-opening prep. Avoiding common oversights saves hours of frustrating troubleshooting later.\n\n1. Measuring the rough opening only once. Concrete framing and basement studs frequently bow. Measure width and height at the top, center, and bottom to ensure the prehung frame actually fits without binding.\n\n2. Placing untreated jamb legs directly on bare concrete. Moisture moves through concrete slabs easily. Placing standard wood directly on damp slabs causes jamb bottoms to rot and swell, throwing the latch out of alignment. Use pressure-treated lumber or moisture breaks.\n\n3. Neglecting hinge-side shims. Fastening directly into rough studs without shimming leaves hollow gaps. Over-tightening pulls the jamb out of plumb, creating a door that swings open on its own or rubs the floor.\n\n4. Overfilling gaps with aggressive expanding foam. Using high-expansion foam around the jamb bows the jamb inward, pinching the door slab. Always use low-pressure, minimal-expanding window and door foam.\n\n5. Storing doors in damp, unconditioned basements prior to installation. Storing unsealed wood doors on damp slabs causes warping before work even starts.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps:\nWhen dealing with masonry openings, fire-rated assemblies, or heavily sloped concrete floors, hiring a finish contractor protects your finish work. Reach out to Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866 for clean door installations done right.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
